course: dictionary
lesson: 7229
title: Phrases
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A phrase is a small group of words that go together, but it does not make a complete sentence on its own. «a tall green tree» is a phrase. «The tall green tree grew by the river.» is a sentence.
explain: A phrase can also be a common saying. «Not the sharpest knife in the drawer» is a humorous phrase. It does not mean anything about knives. It means someone is not very clever, so you must read the whole group of words to understand it.
explain: Some phrases are used so often that they lose their power. This is called a cliché, such as «It was a dark and stormy night». When you write, try to find a fresh phrase of your own. In maths, «in all» and «how many more» are phrases that tell you what to do.
fact: A phrase is a small group of words that go together but is not a whole sentence.
fact: «In the garden» and «a tall green tree» are phrases, because neither one is a complete sentence.
fact: As a verb, «to phrase» means to say or write something in a particular way, so you can phrase it differently.
fact: A cliché is a phrase used so often that it has lost its power, such as «It was a dark and stormy night».
fact: In music, a phrase is a short part of a tune.
exercise: Which group of words is a phrase, not a whole sentence? | *a tall green tree | The tall tree grew quickly. | Birds sing in the morning. | Ben built a sandcastle.
exercise: «Not the sharpest knife in the drawer» means what? | *Not very clever | A kitchen full of cutlery | A knife that is very sharp
exercise: Which phrase is a cliché? | Maya ate a sandwich at lunch | *It was a dark and stormy night | The dog ran home | Ben built a sandcastle
test: In maths, which phrase tells you to add the numbers together? | Take away | *in all | Shrink | Share between
test: In music, what is a phrase? | A loud drum beat | A type of piano | A whole song from start to finish | *A short part of a tune
exam: Which word means to say or write something in a particular way? | to rhyme | to fold | to sigh | *to phrase
